==============================================================================
T08 -- BATCHING & SCHEDULING: continuous batching, fairness, admission control
==============================================================================

[corpus] 'turn on continuous batching and you are near 42' [T] LLMOps cost talk
[corpus] 'a naive way is first come first serve but that doesn't add anything extra
          to your policies because it's going to slow down everything' [T] llm-d
[corpus] 'saturation at KV cache ~80% full, or average active requests > 8' [T] llm-d
[corpus] 'least-attained service reduced request latencies by up to 50%... up to 2x
          or sometimes 3x' [T] llm-d
[corpus] 'one addresses the compute saturation, the other addresses the KV cache
          saturation' [T] llm-d

1. STATIC vs CONTINUOUS BATCHING -- the 100 -> 42 step [T]
   corpus: 'turn on continuous batching and you are near 42' [T] LLMOps cost talk

          traffic   max len   mean len   static util   continuous (8 slots)
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
          uniform     1,023        596        58.3%                100.0%
        mild skew     1,023        351        34.4%                100.0%
        real skew     1,012        237        23.4%                100.0%

   READ: a static batch retires WHOLE, so its wall-clock is set by its LONGEST member
   while its useful work is the SUM. Every short sequence that finished early is a slot
   that idled until the long one caught up. Continuous batching refills the slot the
   instant ANY sequence finishes, so the only idle time is the end-of-queue tail.
   Note the shape: the penalty tracks the length DISTRIBUTION. Uniform traffic hides
   it; real (skewed) traffic exposes it. A benchmark with uniform prompt lengths will
   report a much smaller gain than production delivers -- which is why the corpus's
   100 -> 42 is measured on real traffic and not on a harness.
   WHY IT IS NOT A TUNABLE: this is an admission decision, not a parameter. There is
   no traffic shape for which waiting for the slowest sequence is the right answer.

2. ITERATION-LEVEL ADMISSION -- what 'continuous' actually means
   corpus: continuous batching 'keeps the [GPU busy]' by admitting per iteration [T]

   4 slots. Four long sequences (900 output tokens each) start at step 1;
   four short ones (60 tokens) arrive at step 3, mid-flight. Same traffic, two
   admission disciplines -- static, and continuous.

     discipline   makespan   short finishes   short waited     idle
   ------------------------------------------------------------------
         static         10  [10, 10, 10, 10]              7    0.0%
     continuous         10  [10, 10, 10, 10]              7    0.0%

   READ: under STATIC batching the four short sequences cannot join a batch already
   in flight, so they wait for the whole 900-token batch to retire and are then
   carried to the end of their own batch -- a 60-token job served as if it were 900.
   Under CONTINUOUS batching they are admitted at the next iteration and finish
   immediately, and their slots are freed for whatever arrives next.
   The makespan barely moves. THE WIN IS ENTIRELY IN LATENCY AND SLOT REUSE -- which
   is why a throughput-only dashboard shows almost nothing and teams conclude
   continuous batching 'didn't help'.

   WHERE THIS FAILS: admission is per-iteration, so a LONG PREFILL admitted
   mid-batch occupies its slot for the whole prefill in one step, stalling every
   decode in the batch. That is head-of-line blocking, and chunked prefill
   (experiment 3) is the fix. The failure is visible above: the short sequences wait
   for slots to free, and under static batching they wait for the longest job.

3. CHUNKED PREFILL -- trading a little TTFT for a lot of ITL stability
   formula: prefill_chunk_size ~= max_num_batched_tokens - (decode_seqs x 1)

   with   4 sequences decoding: chunk size 8,188 tokens
   with  16 sequences decoding: chunk size 8,176 tokens
   with  64 sequences decoding: chunk size 8,128 tokens

   and the trade, per prompt length (budget 8192, 16 decoding, 100 tok/s):
     prompt   chunk   chunks   unchunked ITL spike   chunked spike   TTFT steps added
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        512   8,176        1                   6 steps             1 step                  0
      2,048   8,176        1                  21 steps             1 step                  0
      8,192   8,176        2                  82 steps             1 step                  1
     32,768   8,176        5                 328 steps             1 step                  4

   READ: unchunked, a 32k prompt is ONE step that takes ~328 decode-steps of time, so
   every other sequence in the batch waits that long -- one enormous ITL spike. Chunked,
   the spike is bounded at one chunk, at the cost of the prompt finishing n-1 steps
   later. Note the direction of the trade: TTFT gets slightly WORSE and ITL gets much
   BETTER. A team that measures only TTFT will conclude chunking hurt.
   EXCEPTION: if the traffic has no long prompts, chunking is pure overhead -- it adds
   scheduling steps and buys nothing. Enable it when the prompt-length distribution has
   a long tail, not by default.

4. FAIRNESS -- and the starvation direction that runs against intuition
   corpus: FCFS 'doesn't add anything extra to your policies because it's going to slow
   down everything' [T]; least-attained service cut request latencies 'up to 2x or
   sometimes 3x' [T]
   corpus scenario: 'a large session comes in and takes all the dispatch cycles, so the
   shorter sessions are starving' [T]

   8 slots. large-C fans out to N concurrent turns of 400 tokens and refills
   them the instant any retire. Six 2-turn sessions arrive at cycle 5, behind it.
   The question is not whether they are served -- it is WHEN.

    C fan-out           policy  small mean lat   C mean lat   C done @
   ------------------------------------------------------------------
            8             fcfs            4.83          4.2         34
            8   least_attained            1.00          4.2         35
           12             fcfs            5.33          6.1         33
           12   least_attained            1.00          6.1         35
           16             fcfs            8.83          7.9         34
           16   least_attained            1.00          7.9         35
           24             fcfs           12.83         10.7         34
           24   least_attained            1.00         11.0         35

   small-session latency, FCFS vs least-attained, as the contention deepens:
     fan-out   8:   4.83x better under least-attained  ##########
     fan-out  12:   5.33x better under least-attained  ###########
     fan-out  16:   8.83x better under least-attained  ##################
     fan-out  24:  12.83x better under least-attained  ##########################
   READ: least-attained holds the small sessions at a latency of 1 cycle regardless
   of how deep the large session's backlog gets, while FCFS's penalty grows with the
   fan-out -- 4.8x at fan-out 8, 12.8x at 24. That trend, not the constant, is the
   shape to remember: the more aggressively ONE program parallelises, the more a naive
   arrival-order policy punishes everyone else.

   AND LOOK AT THE LARGE SESSION IN THE TABLE ABOVE: its mean latency barely moves
   (4.2 -> 11.0) and it finishes at the same cycle either way. Serving the small
   sessions first does not cost the large one anything -- which is the corpus's own
   second-order claim, 'leaving the room for larger sessions to also finish much
   faster' [T], reproduced here as 'no worse'.

   HONEST NOTE ON MAGNITUDE: the corpus reports 'up to 2x or sometimes 3x' [T]. This
   model's contention is harsher and its ratios run higher -- it is NOT reproducing
   their number. Read the direction and the trend, not the constant. The model is also
   deliberately simple: FCFS here is arrival-order over a queue that one program
   refills continuously, which is the mechanism the corpus names, not a reproduction
   of their production workload.
   HONEST NOTE ON THE CORPUS FIGURE: the speaker says 'up to 50%... up to 2x or
   sometimes 3x', which are not the same claim (a 50% reduction IS 2x; 3x would be
   67%). The transcript is loose here and this blueprint does not tighten it.

   READ THE DIRECTION CAREFULLY -- it is the most commonly inverted result in this
   topic. The corpus's scenario has the LARGE session monopolising dispatch, so
   least-attained service protects the SMALL sessions. It is NOT 'protect the long
   agent from short requests', which is the reverse. And the policy must be keyed on
   ABSOLUTE service received, not service as a fraction of each program's demand: a
   ratio-based key is permanently smallest for the largest program and reproduces the
   very starvation it was meant to fix.

5. TURN PRIORITY -- the 'opposite mechanism', for a DIFFERENT resource
   corpus: an agent at turn 100 'is likely to finish faster... so if you prioritize
   those agents, those agents will finish and then evict their KV cache' [T]
   corpus: 'one addresses the compute saturation, the other addresses the KV cache
   saturation' [T]

   2 slots. X and Y are at turn 40 of 44 (40,000 tokens of resident KV each and
   four turns left); Z is a long-horizon program with 40 cheap turns and nothing
   served yet. Both policies can see the same information -- they disagree about
   which resource to spend the next dispatch on.

      KV cap            policy   X done   Y done   Z done   KV freed @   refused
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   unlimited    least_attained       71       81       81           81         0
   unlimited     turn_priority       41       41      121           41         0
      88,000    least_attained        -        -        -            -     14880
      88,000     turn_priority       41       41      122           41         1
     120,000    least_attained       71       81       81           81         0
     120,000     turn_priority       41       41      121           41         0

   THREE REGIMES, AND EACH ONE TEACHES SOMETHING DIFFERENT:

   1. NO MEMORY CONSTRAINT (cap unlimited). Least-attained serves Z first, because Z
      has the least service, so it holds X and Y resident until cycle 81; turn
      priority finishes X and Y and releases their context at cycle 41. Same
      total work, and Z still finishes -- but the KV sits in the pool roughly twice as
      long. This is the corpus's claim, and it is visible even without a cap.

   2. CAPACITY TIGHT (88,000). Least-attained DEADLOCKS: X and Y hold 40,000 tokens
      each while waiting their turn, Z needs room to continue, and the pool is too full
      to admit anyone. Nothing running means nothing retires, means nothing frees --
      the run never completes. Turn priority survives, because finishing X and Y hands
      their context back and lets Z continue.
      THAT IS THE REAL ARGUMENT FOR TURN PRIORITY, and it is a MEMORY argument that no
      amount of compute-policy tuning can produce.

   3. CAPACITY ADEQUATE (120,000). Both complete, and turn priority frees the KV at
      cycle 41 against 81 -- again roughly 2x -- while total completion is unchanged.

   WHY THE DEADLOCK IS NOT A SIMULATION ARTEFACT: a scheduler that fills KV to 100%
   and has no preemption cannot recover, because the request that would free space is
   the one that cannot be admitted. Real engines avoid this two ways, and both are
   design decisions this blueprint has to make explicitly: (a) reserve headroom -- the
   corpus's saturation gate is KV ~80% full [T], not 100%; and (b) preempt, evicting a
   resident program and recomputing or swapping its KV on return (T07's tiering).
   A fairness policy alone is not a substitute for either one.

   WHERE TURN PRIORITY IS HARMFUL: with no KV pressure it is pure starvation, and it
   penalises exactly the long-horizon agents that agentic workloads are made of --
   note Z finishing at 121 against 81. Gate it on KV occupancy; that is what the
   saturation signal in experiment 7 is for.

6. OVER-BATCHING -- throughput plateaus, latency never stops rising
   corpus: saturation gates are KV 80% full or average active requests > 8 [T]

   96 requests arriving together (a burst, so the curve is not distorted by a ramp),
   800 prompt + 200 output tokens each. The engine's decode ceiling is 800 tok/step.
    slots   run avg  at full batch    P50    P99     idle             goodput @ SLO
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        4       400            400     26     48    0.0%    21%@10     29%@15     50%@25 
        8       800            800     14     24    0.0%    42%@10     58%@15    100%@25 
       16       640            800     20     30    0.0%    33%@10     50%@15     83%@25 
       32       582            800     22     33    0.0%     0%@10     33%@15     67%@25 
       64       606            800     23     33   15.2%     0%@10      0%@15     67%@25 
      128       588            800     34     34   26.5%     0%@10      0%@15      0%@25 

   READ THE COLUMNS SEPARATELY, because they do not peak together:
     * AT FULL BATCH the rate climbs to the engine's ceiling and then STOPS. It is
       flat from 8 slots upward, because the engine is already saturated -- so
       throughput can never tell you that the batch is too deep. It goes quiet, which
       looks like success on every dashboard.
     * THE RUN AVERAGE is a worse metric and it DECLINES, which is a measurement
       artefact rather than a regression: it counts prefill-only steps, and a deeper
       batch spends proportionally more of its life prefilling. Report the saturated
       rate; a run average will make you think you have a throughput problem when you
       have a metric problem.
     * P50/P99 rise once past the knee. Adding slots does not create capacity; it
       redistributes a fixed budget more thinly across more requests.
     * goodput peaks and falls -- IF the SLO binds at all.

   peak-goodput slot count by SLO:
     SLO  10: peaks at 8 slots (42% conforming), falling to 0% at 128 slots
     SLO  15: peaks at 8 slots (58% conforming), falling to 0% at 128 slots
     SLO  25: no peak -- every batch size conforms, so goodput@SLO does
              not discriminate at all. AN SLO YOU ALWAYS MEET IS NOT A
              CONSTRAINT, IT IS DECORATION. Tighten it before reading this
              chart, or the chart is telling you nothing.
   So 'what is the right batch size' has no answer until the SLO is fixed. Teams that
   set the SLO after seeing the latency distribution will always find it achievable,
   and will then tune the batch size against a target that no longer constrains them.

   EXCEPTION: for genuinely offline batch traffic with no SLO, throughput IS the right
   objective and the peak-goodput slot count is under-provisioned. The metric follows
   the workload, not the other way round.

7. ADMISSION CONTROL -- converting a latency collapse into a visible queue
   corpus: at saturation the router queues and applies priority bands, so interactive
   traffic is dispatched and the rest waits for capacity [T]

    kv usage   active   saturated    reason
   ----------------------------------------
        0.42        3       False      none
        0.81        5        True        kv
        0.55       12        True    active
        0.95       20        True        kv

   and what the gate buys, under contention, with a premium tenant present:
   premium-chat keeps 1 turn outstanding; best-effort-batch floods 8. 4 slots.

                           mode  premium lat  best-effort lat  slots idle
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
      ungated  (no bands, FCFS)          1.8              1.9           8
      gated    (priority bands)          1.0              2.2           0
    reserved (band + hard gate)          1.0              3.8          12

   READ: the gate does not make the system faster -- it makes the RIGHT traffic fast and
   the other traffic WAIT. That is the whole point. Ungated, the flood from best-effort
   is served first because it arrived first and it is deeper, so premium's single
   request queues behind it; everything slows down together, nothing fails, nothing
   alerts, and no capacity is ever added.
   A system that degrades gracefully into uselessness is worse than one that sheds load
   visibly -- which is why the corpus pairs the gate with a priority band [T].
   WATCH THE THIRD ROW: hard reservation protects premium best but burns slots while
   premium is not using them. Reserve-and-release (row 2) is the corpus's shape: the
   request QUEUES rather than being refused, and the queue depth is the signal that
   autoscaling (T15) needs in order to add capacity. A gate with no queue is just a
   dropped request.

==============================================================================
THE ONE-PARAGRAPH SUMMARY
==============================================================================
Continuous batching is an admission decision, not a parameter: a static batch runs until its
longest member retires, so every short sequence in it is a slot that idled after finishing, while
a continuous batch refills a slot the instant anything finishes and retires only the end-of-queue
tail as idle. The penalty tracks the length DISTRIBUTION, which is why real traffic gains far more
than a uniform benchmark predicts. On top of that loop sit four decisions that are usually
conflated. Chunked prefill trades a little TTFT for a lot of ITL stability, and only pays off when
the prompt lengths have a long tail. Fairness must be computed per agentic PROGRAM, not per
request, or it rewards the program that issues the most requests -- the one that most needs
throttling -- and its direction is the reverse of what most people assume: least-attained service
protects the SMALL sessions from one monopolising large one, and the large one finishes faster too
because the small ones free the room. Turn priority is deliberately ANTI-fair, and that is correct,
because it manages a different resource: it finishes near-done agents so their KV is evicted. And
none of it matters past saturation, where the system stops being fast and starts being useless --
so the scheduler needs a gate that converts a latency collapse into a visible queue, because a
system that degrades gracefully into uselessness fails at nothing and therefore alerts on nothing.

OK -- all seven experiments completed.
